## learn-cuda

This repo will be used to document my first pass through learning CUDA. I will try and keep all code as well-documented as possible for the sake of others (but mostly myself :))

## Dev environment

`Dockerfile` / `docker-compose.yml` set up a CUDA 13.0 + Python 3.12 + PyTorch 2.13.0 (`+cu130`) dev container. The image is published to Docker Hub as `siddharthmmani/learn-cuda:2.13.0-cu130` (linux/amd64).

### Local (Apple Silicon / any host)

```
docker compose build   # builds from the Dockerfile
docker compose up -d
docker compose exec cuda-dev bash
```

No GPU passthrough on macOS — this is fine for compiling/checking code, not for running kernels.

### Remote GPU workspace (x86_64 + NVIDIA GPU)

Before pulling, check the remote host meets both prerequisites — neither is baked into the image, since both are host-side:

1. **NVIDIA driver** supports CUDA 13.0 (Linux driver ≳ 580.x). Check with `nvidia-smi` — if it fails or reports an old driver, `torch.cuda.is_available()` / kernel launches will fail with a "CUDA driver version is insufficient" error.
2. **NVIDIA Container Toolkit** is installed and Docker is configured with the `nvidia` runtime, otherwise the container starts but no GPU is visible to it.

Once both check out:

```
docker compose pull    # fetches siddharthmmani/learn-cuda:2.13.0-cu130 instead of building
docker compose up -d
docker compose exec cuda-dev python3 -c "import torch; print(torch.cuda.is_available())"
```

Should print `True`.